WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.200
How do nondeterministic automata combine

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.920
languages by concatenation and star?

3
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.300
Epsilon arrows let a branch

4
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.380
choose where one factor ends

5
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.440
and the next begins without

6
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.960
consuming a symbol. We will

7
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:17.060
build the constructions and expose

8
00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.640
why star needs a fresh

9
00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.020
start state.

10
00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.280
For concatenation, take a machine

11
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.740
for the first language and

12
00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:26.400
one for the second. Keep

13
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.200
the first start, add epsilon

14
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.340
arrows from each first-machine final

15
00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:31.920
to the second start, and

16
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.660
make only the second-machine finals

17
00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.860
accepting. The new arrows guess

18
00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:36.940
a split of the input

19
00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.100
into a first-language prefix and

20
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.280
a second-language suffix.

21
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.960
Use the tutorial machines: the

22
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.900
first recognizes words ending in

23
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.780
a, and the second recognizes

24
00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.380
even-length words. The word a

25
00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:50.740
b a has the split

26
00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:52.140
a followed by b a,

27
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.120
so an accepting branch can

28
00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.540
leave the first machine after

29
00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:57.280
its initial a. The second

30
00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.120
machine consumes two symbols and

31
00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.000
finishes accepting.

32
00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.360
The first component’s old finals

33
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.100
must not remain finals of

34
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.900
the combined machine unless the

35
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.720
construction provides an accepting second

36
00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.480
run. Old arrows are retained,

37
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:10.960
so another branch may delay

38
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.180
the split. This choice handles

39
00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.300
every possible factorization instead of

40
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.720
committing greedily to the first

41
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:17.680
apparent opportunity.

42
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.860
For correctness, start with an

43
00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.440
accepted concatenation run and locate

44
00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.780
the epsilon crossing between components.

45
00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.220
The consumed prefix belongs to

46
00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:29.740
the first language and the

47
00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:31.180
remaining suffix to the second.

48
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.160
Conversely, accepting runs on any

49
00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:35.940
such prefix and suffix can

50
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:36.980
be joined by the new

51
00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:37.920
epsilon arrow.

52
00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.480
Star allows zero or more

53
00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:42.980
factors from one language. Add

54
00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:44.660
a fresh accepting start with

55
00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:46.080
an epsilon arrow to the

56
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.220
old start, and epsilon arrows

57
00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.940
from old finals back to

58
00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.240
the old start. Keep the

59
00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.340
old finals accepting too, so

60
00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:55.660
the run may stop after

61
00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:56.980
any completed factor.

62
00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.120
Why not simply make the

63
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.320
old start accepting? Consider the

64
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:02.580
machine whose start moves to

65
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:03.780
a final on a and

66
00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.020
whose final returns to the

67
00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:06.880
start on b, recognizing a

68
00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.320
followed by repetitions of b

69
00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.900
a. Declaring the old start

70
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.260
final would accept a b

71
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.380
even though every nonempty concatenation

72
00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:14.720
of original words ends in

73
00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:15.000
a.

74
00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.480
The fresh start avoids that

75
00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.220
error because no old transition

76
00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:20.920
can revisit it midway through

77
00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.020
a word. It supplies epsilon

78
00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:24.980
for zero factors without changing

79
00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.780
which old prefixes count as

80
00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:29.540
completed factors. The returning epsilon

81
00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.580
arrows then join only genuinely

82
00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:33.100
accepted original words.

83
00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.100
An accepting star run can

84
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.260
be cut at its restart

85
00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:39.060
arrows into accepted factors. Conversely,

86
00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.520
concatenate accepting factor runs using

87
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:42.900
those arrows, with the new

88
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.020
start accepting the zero-factor case.

89
00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.740
This proves closure without claiming

90
00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:50.000
that a nondeterministic branch somehow

91
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.860
knows the right factorization in

92
00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:52.280
advance.

93
00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:53.980
Things to take note of.

94
00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.240
Preserve original arrows, keep component

95
00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.700
state sets disjoint, and distinguish

96
00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:01.600
an epsilon control move from

97
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:04.080
consuming an input symbol. For

98
00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.320
concatenation use the second component’s

99
00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.400
finals; for star use a

100
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.460
fresh accepting start, and prove

101
00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.000
correctness in both directions.
